ach phase.\n\nLooking at Round 0 at APC 300, the 184ms across 2 segments works out to about 92ms per segment, and with roughly 312 AIRs distributed across 8 threads that's about 39 AIRs per thread. If I subtract the expected cell-proportional time of around 15ms per segment, the overhead alone accounts for roughly 77ms per segment. For the MLE rounds, the pattern is similar—82ms per segment at APC 300 versus 24ms per segment at APC 0, but the difference in AIR count per round is substantial, and the kernel execution time for MLE is really driven by the total field element operations.\n\nThe real issue is that MLE Rounds has the worst scaling at 0.72x—163ms at APC 300 despite having 2.35x fewer cells than APC 0's 118ms. With 14 fixed rounds and each round running zerocheck, logup, and interpolation kernels across ~106K columns at APC 300, I need to understand where the per-round overhead is coming from and whether descriptor building or kernel launch costs are the bottleneck.\n\nLooking at the numbers, at APC 300 I have 2 segments with 28 total round iterations, where each round's overhead (descriptor building for 300+ traces, H2D upload, multiple kernel launches) might be 2-3ms per round, totaling around 84ms. Meanwhile at APC 0 with 5 segments and 70 round iterations, the per-round overhead is much lower at ~0.5ms per round since there are only ~20 traces to process, which would total around 35ms of overhead. This suggests the overhead scales with the number of traces rather than cells, which explains why the configuration with fewer but larger segments performs worse.\n\nRather than getting caught up in the exact round counts, which depend on AIR heights, I should focus on what's actually worked before. The most impactful optimizations have been multi-streaming, batching synchronization operations, launching multiple kernels together, tuning GPU page sizes, and moving computation from CPU to GPU—so I should look for similar opportunities in the current bottleneck.\n\nLooking at what hasn't been explored yet, pipelining between segments, fusing multiple MLE operations into a single kernel, overlapping segment processing asynchronously, and reducing NTT overhead all seem worth investigating. The real challenge is that I need to save around 208ms to hit the target at APC 300, which is substantial—no single recent optimization has achieved that much at this scale except the VPMM page size change which saved 186ms.\n\nThe core issue is that GKR fractional sumcheck and MLE sumcheck rounds are inherently sequential, creating a bottleneck that can't be parallelized. However, there's potential to overlap segment processing—at APC 300 there are 2 segments, so if segment 2's early rounds could run concurrently with segment 1's later rounds, that could yield meaningful savings. I need to dig into the prover architecture and understand how segments are currently scheduled to see if this is feasible. Given that the last 10 optimizations have mostly failed and the successful ones saved only 20ms or 6ms, I should be realistic about what's achievable.\n\nThe VPMM page size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB was a massive win at 186ms, and the real benefit came from both reducing cuMemCreate calls and routing smaller allocations through cudaMallocAsync instead of VPMM. When I tried extending this by routing even more allocations through cudaMallocAsync, it regressed—suggesting that VPMM's virtual memory pool provides specific memory layout properties that benefit bandwidth-bound kernels, and disrupting the allocation pattern actually hurts performance. This points to the memory system and allocation strategy having outsized impact on performance, so there might be other similar wins hiding in how memory is managed.\n\nI'm wondering if there are other memory optimization opportunities—like arena allocators for specific phases, pre-allocating known patterns, or warming memory pools—that could yield comparable improvements. Looking at where the biggest gains could come from, LogUp GKR stands out at 358ms now (down from 534ms before the VPMM change), so that phase particularly benefited from the memory optimization.\n\nDigging into the breakdown, the GPU data shows the input eval is multi-streamed across 8 threads but only translates to roughly 60ms wall clock time, yet we're seeing 358ms total—which suggests the fractional sumcheck rounds are consuming most of that time. The individual kernel timings for the sumcheck operations add up quickly across the compute rounds and tree building steps. The fractional sumcheck is the main bottleneck at 233ms of GPU time within the 358ms LogUp GKR phase. Looking at the breakdown across rounds, Round 0 is already heavily optimized but still takes 184ms, the MLE rounds consume 163ms with about 113ms in kernel execution, and Trace Commit dominates at 196ms due to Poseidon2 hashing and merkle tree work. The fractional sumcheck itself processes 14 rounds sequentially, with each round involving compute_round_and_revert operations that I need to examine more closely to find optimization opportunities. each layer's leaves, then D2H transfer of partial results, CPU-side reconstruction of s_evals and Poseidon2 transcript, and finally sending the challenge to the next round.\n\nLooking at the kernel timing data, I'm trying to figure out what's actually per-round versus per-layer in the GKR fractional sumcheck. The bit_rev_frac_build_k2, precompute_m_build_partial, and multifold kernels likely run once per layer rather than per sumcheck round, which would suggest around 6-14 layers total based on their instance counts. The real bottleneck is the sequential dependency within each round: kernel execution, D2H transfer, CPU sponge computation, then generating the next challenge. The earlier attempt to move the CPU sponge onto the GPU hit some limitation, so I need to understand what went wrong there.\n\nNow I'm looking at the Trace Commit phase more carefully. The poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel shows 231ms of GPU time across 25 instances, but the wall clock time for Trace Commit is only 196ms. That's suspicious—there's also calculate_zero_hash, merkle_tree_compress, and other kernels running, so the numbers don't quite add up unless some of these kernels are coming from the recursion phases rather than the initial trace commit.\n\nLet me break down where these kernels are actually coming from. The nsys profiling captures everything across all proving stages: the app proof trace commit, leaf recursion, inner recursion, and compression. So that 231ms of poseidon2 hashing is spread across multiple stages, not just the app proof. If the app proof's Trace Commit is 196ms at APC 300, maybe around 100ms of the poseidon2 work is from the app proof itself, with the rest distributed across the recursion stages.\n\nI'm going to stop diving deeper into the profiling data and focus on concrete optimization ideas instead. The key question is what could realistically save 100+ milliseconds at APC 300.\n\nAfter analyzing 30+ optimization attempts, I'm seeing consistent patterns: the per-AIR overhead scales badly since APC 300 has 6.3x more AIRs, memory bandwidth is the real GPU bottleneck with L2 cache reuse being critical, VPMM pool state creates unpredictable effects on bandwidth-bound kernels, multi-streaming helps when per-AIR work is independent and buffers stay small, and batching reduces overhead but breaks L2 cache locality when applied too broadly.\n\nSo what genuinely new approach could work here? I'm considering whether reducing the LogUp GKR circuit depth would help—it has about 14 layers and each adds sequential work—but that would require a protocol change which isn't allowed. Let me think about what other angles are actually feasible.\n\nWhat if I fused some of the GKR fractional sumcheck kernels together? Each layer currently runs separate kernels like bit_rev_frac_build, precompute_m_build_partial, and compute_round_and_revert multiple times. These operations are independent per-layer and could potentially share memory accesses if combined. Though honestly, the kernel launch overhead savings would only be around 10 microseconds, which is negligible compared to the 8.3ms and 3.2ms execution times of these kernels individually.\n\nActually, there's something more interesting here—the GKR fractional sumcheck processes layers sequentially, but within each layer there are multiple sumcheck rounds, and each round runs compute_round_and_revert across all sub-operations. Maybe I can multi-stream these per-layer kernels to overlap computation across rounds.\n\nLooking at the numbers, compute_round_and_revert appears 141 times total across the proof, which works out to roughly 10 instances per layer given 14 layers with about 10 inner rounds each. These might be able to process different circuit parts concurrently, but I'd need to dig deeper into the actual GKR implementation to understand the dependencies.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the Openings phase—WHIR and Stacked Reduction run back-to-back, taking 125ms and 74ms respectively. If I could overlap them instead of running sequentially, that's potentially 74ms of savings since the combined time could drop from 199ms to around 125ms. The question is whether they share GPU resources that would prevent true overlap. WHIR and Stacked Reduction process different data streams, so running them concurrently could save around 70ms by hiding the shorter operation behind the longer one. For the trace commit phase, it has to happen before constraints since it generates the commitments needed for the transcript, but I could potentially pipeline the work across segments to start committing earlier traces while still processing later ones.\n\nLooking at Round 0 performance at APC 300, I'm seeing 92ms per segment with 184ms total for 2 segments. The GPU kernel time is 560ms across all segments with 8 concurrent streams, which should be around 70ms per stream, but the wall clock time per segment is higher at 92ms — there's some overhead I need to account for.\n\nThe 22ms gap likely comes from CPU work like preparing items, D2H copies, and result extraction, plus potential GPU stream contention since the 8 streams share the same GPU resources and can't all execute perfectly in parallel. I've already eliminated the CPU transpose and iDFT through GPU extraction, so what's left is work item construction, buffer allocation, kernel launch overhead, and copying results back to host.\n\nI'm wondering if I can close that gap and get the per-segment overhead down from 92ms toward the theoretical 70ms, but I need to think through whether the 8 streams are truly concurrent or if GPU SM and memory bandwidth limitations mean they're serializing more than I initially thought.\n\nWith small kernels at APC 300 (maybe 1-4 thread blocks each), 8 streams would only occupy about 25% of the RTX 4090's 128 SMs, so there's definitely room for more parallelism. But increasing from 4 to 8 streams already gave modest gains, suggesting kernel launch overhead might be the real bottleneck at high AIR counts.\n\nLet me step back and focus on what would actually move the needle—I need to save roughly 208ms at APC 300, and looking at the breakdown, LogUp GKR is hard to improve without protocol changes, while Round 0 is already heavily optimized but might still yield 20-40ms through overhead reduction.\n\nThe MLE rounds are kernel-bound with maybe 10-20ms available, the WHIR regression fix could recover 25ms, and stacking the reduction phase with WHIR could overlap and save around 70ms. Even adding all these together—30 plus 30 plus 15 plus 25 plus 70—I'm still about 38ms short of the target. That VPMM page size change that saved 186ms is interesting though; it worked by fundamentally changing memory allocation routing and layout. I'm wondering if there are other memory system tweaks with similar outsized impact that I'm missing.\n\nThe most tractable path forward seems