ructed. height is 2^n_stack where n_stack = log of stacking factor. The RS codeword height is 2^(n_stack + l_skip + log_blowup).\n\nActually, n_stack is the number of stacking levels, which is related to how many AIRs are stacked. With 623 AIRs at APC 300, n_stack = 20 means 2^20 = 1M stacking slots. Each AIR occupies some slots based on its height.\n\nThe stacked height 2^20 = 1M rows. With l_skip = 4 and log_blowup = 1, the codeword height is 2^(20+4+1) = 2^25 = 33M rows. At APC 0 with 99 AIRs, the stacking might need fewer levels, but checking the config shows MAX_APP_LOG_STACKED_HEIGHT = 24 is fixed, so n_stack = 20 regardless of the application configuration. This means the RS encoding and Merkle tree hashing always process the full 33M rows, even though only a fraction of those cells contain actual data.\n\nThe matrix width is the sum of all trace widths. At APC 300 with 623 AIRs, the total width is 106,842 columns. At APC 0 with 99 AIRs, the total width is 3,919 columns. So the RS codeword dimensions are 33M rows × 106K columns for APC 300 and 33M rows × 3.9K columns for APC 0, with the difference in width being the main factor affecting the computational cost.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the per-commit time might not scale linearly with column count. If the Poseidon2 hashing is dominated by the row count rather than the column count, then the per-commit time could be similar across different APC settings even though APC 300 has 27x more columns. But the 196ms Trace Commit time might be breaking down differently than I initially thought—it could include multiple components beyond just the main trace hashing.\n\nI'm overthinking this. Let me settle on a concrete optimization: batching the per-trace stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0 kernel launches into a single descriptor-array kernel. At APC 300, this means launching hundreds of tiny kernels sequentially, which is inefficient. Combining them into one batched kernel call would reduce launch overhead significantly.\