{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012wBN2AXDnPuwdTn1eUoTQz","type":"tool_result","content":"99\timpl<D: Copy + Send + Sync + 'static> MerkleTreeGpu<F, D> {\n100\t    /// Build a Merkle tree using the given hash scheme `MH`.\n101\t    ///\n102\t    /// This is the primary constructor; `new()` is a convenience wrapper that\n103\t    /// fixes `MH = Poseidon2MerkleHash`.\n104\t    #[instrument(name = \"merkle_tree\", skip_all)]\n105\t    pub fn new_with_hash<MH: MerkleTreeConstructor<Digest = D>>(\n106\t        matrix: DeviceMatrix<F>,\n107\t        rows_per_query: usize,\n108\t        cache_backing_matrix: bool,\n109\t    ) -> Result<Self, MerkleTreeError> {\n110\t        MH::new_merkle_tree(matrix, rows_per_query, cache_backing_matrix)\n111\t    }\n112\t\n113\t    fn new_generic_with_hash<MH: GpuMerkleHash<Digest = D>>(\n114\t        matrix: DeviceMatrix<F>,\n115\t        rows_per_query: usize,\n116\t        cache_backing_matrix: bool,\n117\t    ) -> Result<Self, MerkleTreeError> {\n118\t        let mem = MemTracker::start(\"prover.merkle_tree\");\n119\t        let height = matrix.height();\n120\t        assert!(height.is_power_of_two());\n121\t        let k = log2_strict_usize(rows_per_query);\n122\t        assert!(\n123\t            rows_per_query <= height,\n124\t            \"rows_per_query ({rows_per_query}) must not exceed height ({height})\"\n125\t        );\n126\t        let query_stride = height / rows_per_query;\n127\t        let mut query_digest_layer = DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(query_stride);\n128\t        // SAFETY: query_digest_layer properly allocated\n129\t        unsafe {\n130\t            MH::compress_rows(\n131\t                &mut query_digest_layer,\n132\t                matrix.buffer(),\n133\t                matrix.width(),\n134\t                query_stride,\n135\t                k,\n136\t            )\n137\t            .map_err(MerkleTreeError::CompressingRowHashes)?;\n138\t        }\n139\t        // If not caching, drop the backing matrix at this point to save memory.\n140\t        let backing_matrix = cache_backing_matrix.then_some(matrix);\n141\t\n142\t        let mut digest_layers = vec![query_digest_layer];\n143\t        while digest_layers.last().unwrap().len() > 1 {\n144\t            let prev_layer = digest_layers.last().unwrap();\n145\t            let mut layer = DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(prev_layer.len() / 2);\n146\t            let layer_len = layer.len();\n147\t            let layer_idx = digest_layers.len();\n148\t            // SAFETY:\n149\t            // - `layer` is properly allocated with half the size of `prev_layer` and does not\n150\t            //   overlap with it.\n151\t            unsafe {\n152\t                MH::compress_layer(&mut layer, prev_layer, layer_len).map_err(|error| {\n153\t                    MerkleTreeError::AdjacentCompressLayer {\n154\t                        error,\n155\t                        layer: layer_idx,\n156\t                    }\n157\t                })?;\n158\t            }\n159\t            digest_layers.push(layer);\n160\t        }\n161\t        let d_root = digest_layers.last().unwrap();\n162\t        assert_eq!(d_root.len(), 1, \"Only one root is supported\");\n163\t        let root = d_root.to_host()?.pop().unwrap();\n164\t\n165\t        mem.emit_metrics();\n166\t        Ok(Self {\n167\t            backing_matrix,\n168\t            digest_layers,\n169\t            rows_per_query,\n170\t            root,\n171\t        })\n172\t    }\n173\t\n174\t    #[instrument(name = \"batch_open_rows\", skip_all)]\n175\t    pub fn batch_open_rows(\n176\t        backing_matrices: &[&DeviceMatrix<F>],\n177\t        query_indices: &[usize],\n178\t        query_stride: usize,\n179\t        rows_per_query: usize,\n180\t    ) -> Result<\n181\t        Vec<\n182\t            // per tree\n183\t            Vec<\n184\t                // per query index\n185\t                Vec<F>, // opened rows, concatenated for rows_per_query strided rows\n186\t            >,\n187\t        >,\n188\t        MerkleTreeError,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"a8b251cf-e901-4a6b-8b7a-39a55b19f142","timestamp":"2026-04-15T22:47:26.783Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs","content":"impl<D: Copy + Send + Sync + 'static> MerkleTreeGpu<F, D> {\n    /// Build a Merkle tree using the given hash scheme `MH`.\n    ///\n    /// This is the primary constructor; `new()` is a convenience wrapper that\n    /// fixes `MH = Poseidon2MerkleHash`.\n    #[instrument(name = \"merkle_tree\", skip_all)]\n    pub fn new_with_hash<MH: MerkleTreeConstructor<Digest = D>>(\n        matrix: DeviceMatrix<F>,\n        rows_per_query: usize,\n        cache_backing_matrix: bool,\n    ) -> Result<Self, MerkleTreeError> {\n        MH::new_merkle_tree(matrix, rows_per_query, cache_backing_matrix)\n    }\n\n    fn new_generic_with_hash<MH: GpuMerkleHash<Digest = D>>(\n        matrix: DeviceMatrix<F>,\n        rows_per_query: usize,\n        cache_backing_matrix: bool,\n    ) -> Result<Self, MerkleTreeError> {\n        let mem = MemTracker::start(\"prover.merkle_tree\");\n        let height = matrix.height();\n        assert!(height.is_power_of_two());\n        let k = log2_strict_usize(rows_per_query);\n        assert!(\n            rows_per_query <= height,\n            \"rows_per_query ({rows_per_query}) must not exceed height ({height})\"\n        );\n        let query_stride = height / rows_per_query;\n        let mut query_digest_layer = DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(query_stride);\n        // SAFETY: query_digest_layer properly allocated\n        unsafe {\n            MH::compress_rows(\n                &mut query_digest_layer,\n                matrix.buffer(),\n                matrix.width(),\n                query_stride,\n                k,\n            )\n            .map_err(MerkleTreeError::CompressingRowHashes)?;\n        }\n        // If not caching, drop the backing matrix at this point to save memory.\n        let backing_matrix = cache_backing_matrix.then_some(matrix);\n\n        let mut digest_layers = vec![query_digest_layer];\n        while digest_layers.last().unwrap().len() > 1 {\n            let prev_layer = digest_layers.last().unwrap();\n            let mut layer = DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(prev_layer.len() / 2);\n            let layer_len = layer.len();\n            let layer_idx = digest_layers.len();\n            // SAFETY:\n            // - `layer` is properly allocated with half the size of `prev_layer` and does not\n            //   overlap with it.\n            unsafe {\n                MH::compress_layer(&mut layer, prev_layer, layer_len).map_err(|error| {\n                    MerkleTreeError::AdjacentCompressLayer {\n                        error,\n                        layer: layer_idx,\n                    }\n                })?;\n            }\n            digest_layers.push(layer);\n        }\n        let d_root = digest_layers.last().unwrap();\n        assert_eq!(d_root.len(), 1, \"Only one root is supported\");\n        let root = d_root.to_host()?.pop().unwrap();\n\n        mem.emit_metrics();\n        Ok(Self {\n            backing_matrix,\n            digest_layers,\n            rows_per_query,\n            root,\n        })\n    }\n\n    #[instrument(name = \"batch_open_rows\", skip_all)]\n    pub fn batch_open_rows(\n        backing_matrices: &[&DeviceMatrix<F>],\n        query_indices: &[usize],\n        query_stride: usize,\n        rows_per_query: usize,\n    ) -> Result<\n        Vec<\n            // per tree\n            Vec<\n                // per query index\n                Vec<F>, // opened rows, concatenated for rows_per_query strided rows\n            >,\n        >,\n        MerkleTreeError,","numLines":90,"startLine":99,"totalLines":480}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ytNyCKrEh7Ypt2VE5ivzD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the Merkle tree construction:\n1. `compress_rows` — hashes all rows into `query_digest_layer` (one digest per `query_stride` rows)\n2. Then iteratively `compress_layer` until root\n\nThere's no zero-hash optimization. ALL rows are processed, including zero-padded ones.\n\nNow, the key insight: the stacked matrix at APC 300 has height = stacked_height. But in the stacking, shorter traces are zero-padded. The RS codeword extends the height further.\n\nIf we could skip the zero-padded rows during Poseidon2 hashing and instead use a precomputed zero hash, we could save time.\n\n\nBut actually, the zero padding happens per-column in the stacked matrix, not per-row. Each row contains a mix of data from taller traces and zeros from shorter ones, so I can't skip entire rows. The Poseidon2 hashing has to process every row because they all contain some actual data mixed with padding.\n\nThe only fully-zero rows would be above the tallest trace, but since the tallest trace typically spans the full height, there aren't any to skip. The RS extension beyond the trace adds computed values, not zeros. So this optimization path doesn't actually help.\n\nI need to stop going in circles and focus on what actually matters. The real bottleneck is that APC 300 has 6.3x more AIR instances than APC 0, and the prover handles most phases per-AIR. Multi-stream optimizations help but don't close the gap enough. I should explore reducing the number of AIR instances instead.\n\nThe idea is to merge structurally identical AIRs—if multiple APCs share the same trace height and constraints, they could be combined into a single wider AIR with multiple trace column sets. This could cut the AIR count from 623 down to 200-300, significantly reducing per-AIR overhead across all phases. It's a substantial architectural change though, requiring modifications to constraint evaluation and possibly keygen.\n\nActually, stacking already does something similar at the commitment level by combining multiple traces into one matrix, but constraint evaluation still processes AIRs individually. What if I extended stacking to the constraint evaluation itself instead of evaluating each AIR's Round 0 constraints separately?\n\nI could batch AIRs with identical constraint structures—for zerocheck, the kernel would evaluate one constraint polynomial across multiple traces simultaneously, and for logup, AIRs with matching interaction structures could be batched the same way. This could reduce Round 0 kernel launches from around 600 down to 50-100, and it's compatible with the existing multi-stream approach.\n\nThe challenge is that this is complex: I'd need to identify AIR groups by constraint structure, modify the kernel to handle multiple traces for the same constraint, and update the Rust orchestration layer. It's similar in spirit to the failed batch-round0-descriptor-arrays attempts, which tried a different mechanism but had the same fundamental goal.\n\nThe key difference with grouping by constraint structure is that I'd be batching identical constraints on different data rather than different constraints, so the intermediates per AIR stay the same size and can be reused across the group. This avoids the L2 cache thrashing that killed the descriptor-array approach, since all AIRs in a group share the same constraint complexity. The previous attempts failed due to kernel bugs with >12 AIRs and aggregate intermediates that were too large, but focusing on a specific subset of constraints might sidestep those issues.\n\nLooking at the metrics, I see that only 2 AIR types have zero constraints while 363 have at least one, meaning roughly 99.5% of AIRs have constraints. So the optimization of skipping zerocheck entirely for constraint-free AIRs isn't practical—nearly everything needs that kernel anyway.\n\nI'm pivoting to a different approach: pre-computing the descriptor arrays for all MLE rounds upfront instead of rebuilding them each iteration. Since the descriptors only change in size due to folding but keep the same structure, I can compute them once before the MLE loop starts rather than iterating through ~312 traces per round across 28 iterations. Though I'm realizing this is similar to what the \"precompute-mle-fold-layout\" attempt already explored.\n\nLooking back at that attempt, it replaced 112 per-round device transfers and 28 kernel launches with 4 bulk uploads and 14 fused launches, but the improvement was negligible—only 0.3-0.5ms saved, which is below the measurement noise. So that path isn't worth revisiting.\n\nI need to step back and be honest about where I stand: the easy wins are gone, what's left are either modest gains of 10-30ms or complex risky changes, and I've already achieved a 2.22x speedup against baseline. The remaining 208ms gap to hit the 2x APC0/APC300 ratio target is substantial, but I'm hitting diminishing returns.\n\nThe one idea I haven't fully explored is pre-allocating and reusing the stacked commit matrix across segments instead of creating and destroying it for each one. If that large matrix allocation through VPMM takes 20-30ms, reusing it could save that time per segment. Though the memory pool might already be handling reuse efficiently, so the actual savings could be much smaller than expected.\n\nI'm settling on optimizing the Trace Commit phase by multi-streaming the RS code matrix computation. The NTT and iNTT operations process a huge number of columns at APC 300, and splitting them into independent groups across multiple CUDA streams could overlap computation with memory transfers. Though I need to verify whether NTT can actually be parallelized this way without dependencies between column groups.\n\nActually, the batch NTT already processes all columns in parallel on the GPU, so additional streaming won't help—the GPU is already saturated. That won't work.\n\nLet me try reducing per-AIR overhead by precomputing kernel parameters on the CPU and uploading them as a packed buffer instead of doing per-AIR setup. But that's mostly just pointer arithmetic and conditionals, not the real bottleneck.\n\nI'm going to try overlapping the Merkle tree root extraction—which is a D2H sync point—with GKR input evaluation preparation. After Trace Commit builds the tree, the root hash gets extracted to host memory, and that's where I can hide latency.\n\nBut the root is needed for the Fiat-Shamir transcript before challenges are sampled, and GKR prep depends on those challenges. So deferring the root extraction just delays everything downstream.\n\nWhat if I move the transcript operations to the GPU entirely? If the root hash is observed and challenges are sampled on the GPU using a GPU-side Poseidon2 sponge, I could avoid the D2H sync for the root altogether. I explored this for GKR before...\n\nActually, looking back at that experiment, the per-round savings were minimal because the CPU sponge is already fast. For a single Merkle root observation, the gains would be even smaller.\n\nLet me take a different approach and examine how the prover coordinates work across segments, looking for actual overlap opportunities between when one segment finishes and the next begins.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6539,"cache_read_input_tokens":144860,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6539},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"33b4d08e-8804-4a64-a278-a6c3907e70a0"}
