Batching all those launches into a single kernel would only save about 6 milliseconds total—below what would justify the optimization effort.\n\nSo the real bottleneck isn't the stacked reduction kernel batching itself.\n\nThe actual blocker is that APC 100 and APC 300 measurements are hitting GPU out-of-memory errors during GKR input evaluation, even on the unmodified codebase after CUDA rebuilds. This OOM is preventing any testing of the batch Round 0 optimization that's already been implemented. Solving this would unblock future optimization work, but it's not strictly a STARK exclusion trace optimization. That said, if the OOM stems from bloated cubin or fatbin sizes due to template instantiations, reducing templates could free up GPU memory and indirectly improve STARK exclusion trace performance.\n\nThe PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS flag already demonstrated this principle—it shrank cubin from 9.3M to 6.5M, which frees memory for proving workloads, reduces CUDA context initialization overhead, and enables larger VPMM pools. While cubin reduction isn't a direct performance optimization, lower GPU memory pressure benefits memory-bandwidth-bound kernels. The real opportunity is overlapping the Stacked Reduction Round 0 CPU reconstruction with the subsequent fold_ple_evals GPU computation to eliminate idle time between these phases.\n\nCurrently the pipeline drains after GPU kernels finish, then CPU NTT reconstruction runs sequentially before fold_ple_evals launches. But since fold_ple_evals' GPU work doesn't depend on the reconstruction output, I can start those kernels while the CPU is still reconstructing, hiding the CPU latency entirely.\n\nActually, wait—the transcript observation of s_0 has to happen before u_0 is sampled, and u_0 is what fold_ple_evals needs. So there's a hard dependency chain: reconstruction → observe → sample → fold. They can't overlap.\n\nUnless I move the entire reconstruction and sampling pipeline to the GPU. Then all the work stays on-device: reconstruct s_0 with a tiny GPU NTT kernel, observe it into the transcript sponge on GPU, sample u_0 from the sponge on GPU, and immediately launch fold_ple_evals. That eliminates the D2H transfer and CPU bottleneck entirely.\n\nBut I've seen this before—GPU-side transcript processing didn't help much because the CPU work was already negligible. Even if reconstruction takes 5-10ms and the D2H sync adds another 5ms, that's only ~10ms of potential savings. Not compelling enough to justify the complexity.\n\nLet me step back and look at what's actually causing the WHIR regression instead.\n\nThe baseline was 100ms at APC 300, now it's 125ms—that's a 25ms hit. At APC 0 there's barely any change. If I can isolate and fix what's driving this regression, I get 25ms back without affecting the other case. The issue seems to be accumulated memory pool state changes from earlier phases, not the fold buffer allocation itself. I'm wondering if the problem actually stems from how memory gets allocated in the rounds before WHIR runs.\n\nLooking at the options: I could defragment the memory pool between phases, pre-allocate WHIR's buffers early to claim good memory locations, or spin up a fresh stream with its own pool. The pre-allocation approach is promising—if I know WHIR's buffer sizes ahead of time based on matrix dimensions and sumcheck rounds, I can grab that memory before constraints even start, which should give WHIR better memory placement for its bandwidth-sensitive kernels.\n\nThe tricky part is that WHIR parameters get finalized during the openings phase, but actually, looking at the code, those parameters depend on stacked codeword dimensions and l_skip/n_stack values that are already known at keygen time. So I can predict the buffer sizes upfront and pre-allocate them before the constraint phase runs. The earlier attempt tried something similar but allocated the scratch buffers too late—right at the start of the WHIR function itself, which was after the memory pool had already been fragmented by prior allocations.\n\nThe real win would be allocating these buffers before constraints even start, when the memory pool is still clean. Then when WHIR runs, its buffers are already in optimal positions. This could save 15-25ms on WHIR at APC 300, though there's a medium probability it actually helps—depends on whether the timing genuinely improves memory layout. The complexity is moderate: compute sizes at keygen, allocate before constraints, pass them through to WHIR.\n\nBut there's a real risk here. Previous attempts at manipulating the memory pool state have caused cascading regressions in other phases.\n\nInstead of pre-allocating specific buffers, what if I just reset the CUDA memory pool to a clean state right before WHIR starts? I could synchronize the stream and trim the pool to release freed pages back to the driver. That might eliminate the pool state sensitivity entirely. Though trimming could lose pre-allocated pages and slow down subsequent allocations, and the driver's page allocator might just hand back the same physical pages anyway.\n\nLet me step back and look at which specific WHIR kernels actually regressed.\n\nNow I'm comparing the baseline nsys data against the current results to identify which kernels are slower.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":309,"cache_read_input_tokens":158782,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":309},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"8ff5e917-3339-4385-83bf-c3a96b6b5939"}
