{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HCxGRemMMBptDyJoYGhWUN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Grind kernel times are also nearly identical (15.9ms vs 16.3ms). So the 25ms WHIR regression is entirely from CPU-side or allocation overhead, not from GPU kernels.\n\nNow, looking at the WHIR inner loop:\n1. `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round` — GPU kernel launch\n2. `d_s_evals.to_host()` — D2H sync (pipeline drain)\n3. `transcript.observe_ext(...)` — CPU transcript (fast)\n4. `transcript.grind_gpu(...)` — GPU grind kernel\n\n\n5. `alpha = transcript.sample_ext()` — CPU sampling\n6. `whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments(...)` — GPU kernel\n7. `new_f_coeffs` and `new_w_moments` allocation — may trigger VPMM\n8. Old buffers dropped — may trigger VPMM free\n\nThe synchronization point at step 2 and the allocation/deallocation overhead in steps 7-8 are the culprits. With k_whir = 4 and num_whir_rounds = 4 or 5, that's 16-20 inner rounds total, so the sync and allocation costs compound across all those iterations.\n\nThe 25ms regression breaks down to roughly 1.25-1.56ms of overhead per round. Looking at the allocation pattern, new_f_coeffs and new_w_moments get allocated at the start of each inner round, with old buffers freed when replaced. Since these buffers are 8 MB and 4 MB respectively—both well below the 16 MiB VPMM threshold—they're going through cudaMallocAsync rather than VPMM, so the overhead must be coming from somewhere else in the allocation path or the synchronization itself.\n\nThe `d_s_evals.to_host()` call does a small D2H copy and syncs the stream, but if the kernel timing is consistent, the sync overhead shouldn't change. However, the VPMM page size change might have introduced additional driver bookkeeping during synchronization, or the `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` calls could be the culprit.\n\nActually, I think the real issue is that `d_s_evals.to_host()` drains the pipeline and waits for async operations from the previous inner round, including the async freeing of old buffers. With the larger page size, those cuMemUnmap and cuMemRelease operations on large buffers are now more expensive. Let me check the CUDA API profile data to see how cuMemUnmap calls compare between the baseline and current runs.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2037,"cache_read_input_tokens":169347,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2037},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"0ba290bf-4c44-41c3-982b-90ad73a4163b"}
