{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013QL8q2KKGi2DjPHtKqaQjx","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"d55e2696-e584-4bca-8288-8b13f86a0b07","timestamp":"2026-04-15T22:54:44.967Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-0030-reuse-whir-buffers-inplace-fold\n\nReduce WHIR opening proof overhead by reusing f_coeffs and w_moments buffers across outer WHIR rounds (avoiding re-allocation when capacity suffices) and folding in-place within inner rounds (eliminating per-round allocation of new output buffers). Nsight profiling confirms WHIR GPU kernel times are identical between baseline (46ms) and current (46ms), but wall clock regressed from 100ms to 125ms — the 25ms regression is entirely from non-kernel overhead (allocation churn, sync latency). This optimization targets the allocation-related portion of that overhead.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-15-2230-prealloc-whir-fold-buffers","+# 2026-04-16-0030-reuse-whir-buffers-inplace-fold"," ","-Pre-allocate and reuse WHIR sumcheck fold buffers (`f_coeffs`, `w_moments`) via a pingpong pattern instead of allocating new DeviceBuffers each inner round. Currently, each of the k_whir × num_whir_rounds inner sumcheck rounds allocates two fresh DeviceBuffers (one for folded `f_coeffs`, one for folded `w_moments`) and frees the previous pair. For the first ~5 rounds at APC 300 (buffer sizes 128-16 MiB), these go through VPMM, creating page churn that disturbs GPU memory pool state for subsequent bandwidth-bound phases. The WHIR phase regressed 25ms from baseline (100ms → 125ms) coinciding with VPMM page size changes, suggesting pool state sensitivity. Pre-allocating at maximum size and alternating between two buffer pairs eliminates all per-round VPMM allocations/frees and stabilizes pool state.","+Reduce WHIR opening proof overhead by reusing f_coeffs and w_moments buffers across outer WHIR rounds (avoiding re-allocation when capacity suffices) and folding in-place within inner rounds (eliminating per-round allocation of new output buffers). Nsight profiling confirms WHIR GPU kernel times are identical between baseline (46ms) and current (46ms), but wall clock regressed from 100ms to 125ms — the 25ms regression is entirely from non-kernel overhead (allocation churn, sync latency). This optimization targets the allocation-related portion of that overhead."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-15-2230-prealloc-whir-fold-buffers\n\nPre-allocate and reuse WHIR sumcheck fold buffers (`f_coeffs`, `w_moments`) via a pingpong pattern instead of allocating new DeviceBuffers each inner round. Currently, each of the k_whir × num_whir_rounds inner sumcheck rounds allocates two fresh DeviceBuffers (one for folded `f_coeffs`, one for folded `w_moments`) and frees the previous pair. For the first ~5 rounds at APC 300 (buffer sizes 128-16 MiB), these go through VPMM, creating page churn that disturbs GPU memory pool state for subsequent bandwidth-bound phases. The WHIR phase regressed 25ms from baseline (100ms → 125ms) coinciding with VPMM page size changes, suggesting pool state sensitivity. Pre-allocating at maximum size and alternating between two buffer pairs eliminates all per-round VPMM allocations/frees and stabilizes pool state.\n"}}
