ion to work without conflicts, I need to verify that when Output[k] writes to index k, no later output thread still needs that value. Output[j] reads indices 2*j and 2*j+1, so a conflict occurs only if k equals 2*j or 2*j+1, meaning j = k/2 or j = (k-1)/2. Since these values of j are always less than k, there's no actual conflict—each write happens after all dependent reads are complete.\n\nHowever, on a GPU, the execution order matters. Threads in the same warp execute in lockstep with reads happening before writes in the same instruction, so they're safe. But if thread k and thread k/2 are in different warps, thread k might write to index k before thread k/2 finishes reading it, corrupting the data.\n\nLooking at a concrete example: if k = 32 and j = 16, and warp 1 executes before warp 0, thread 32 writes to index 32 before thread 16 reads from it, destroying the original value. This means in-place folding isn't safe on a standard GPU kernel—we'd need either separate read/write passes, a pingpong buffer approach, or explicit warp synchronization. The pingpong strategy was actually tested and reverted because it only provided a 4ms improvement.\n\nBut what if I pre-allocate two scratch buffers at maximum size upfront and reuse them across all folding rounds? Instead of allocating new buffers each iteration, I'd just swap pointers between the current coefficients and the scratch buffers. This keeps the memory overhead constant at exactly 2 extra buffers while avoiding the allocation overhead that made the previous attempt inefficient.\n\nActually, looking back at the prealloc-whir-fold-buffers results, that's essentially what was already tried—pre-allocating 256 MiB scratch buffers only saved 4ms. The real issue might be that allocating such massive buffers upfront creates memory pressure on the GPU, especially since the report shows no regression at APC 0 but the gains were minimal overall.\n\nThe 4ms savings came from eliminating about 6 VPMM allocations across th