coming from WHIR's STARK excluding the trace.\n\nThat would put WHIR's GPU time around 132ms total, but the wall clock shows 125ms, so I'm likely overestimating how much of those operations belong to WHIR. Let me reconsider the actual structure—the first outer round operates on a massive codeword of 131K rows by 4 columns, and each subsequent round shrinks exponentially. The poseidon2 row hashing for that initial matrix alone would be substantial.\n\nBut I'm realizing most of the 125ms wall clock time might actually be spent in query opening—extracting specific rows, transferring them from GPU to CPU, and generating the Merkle proofs by walking the tree. That's where the real bottleneck likely sits.\n\nLooking at the profiling data, the w_moments_accumulate kernel (6.6ms across 14 instances) and whir_algebraic_batch (18ms across 6 instances) are clearly WHIR-specific operations, but they're not the dominant cost. Rather than chasing smaller optimizations like buffer allocation, I should focus on reducing the Merkle tree construction overhead during the query opening phase.\n\nEach outer round rebuilds a Merkle tree over the folded codeword, which involves allocating digest layer buffers, running RS encoding kernels, and executing multiple compression layers. Since this happens once per outer round, the cumulative cost scales with the number of rounds. My optimization strategy is to pre-allocate these digest layer buffers once and reuse them across all WHIR outer rounds, eliminating the repeated allocation overhead.\n\nHowever, calculating the actual savings—roughly 85 cudaMallocAsync calls at ~1μs each—yields only about 85 microseconds of improvement, which is negligible. This suggests the 25ms WHIR regression isn't coming from the Merkle tree allocation pattern itself, but rather from some broader systemic change in how the computation is structured.\n\nLet me pivot away from chasing WHIR optimizations and try a different angle. The most practical approach is to fi