tly GPU kernel execution time itself, and I need to accept that modest improvements might be the best I can achieve.\n\nThe more ambitious approach would be to pipeline the outer loop operations—specifically, overlap RS encoding and Merkle tree construction with the subsequent sumcheck rounds, so these expensive operations don't block the critical path. I see the dependency issue now—evaluating the polynomial at z_0 requires the coefficients that the background stream is still processing, so I can't actually parallelize those steps. The real overlap opportunity is narrower: I can run the RS encoding and Merkle tree construction on a background stream while the main thread handles the evaluation and transcript work, but only if the polynomial coefficients are ready first. After step 2, g_coeffs is ready, but z_0 doesn't arrive until step 7, so step 8 can't start early. The critical dependency chain is 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6 → 7 → 8, with no parallelization within a single round. Across rounds, round i's query work can't overlap with round i+1's sumcheck because the next round depends on the transcript updates from the previous round's queries. I've hit a wall on parallelization opportunities, so I should focus on a practical plan around WHIR buffer reuse and acknowledge the inherent sequential constraints.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2743,"cache_read_input_tokens":203549,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2743},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"6b763341-9f42-4c61-a95d-30176a90edca"}
