duces any new bottlenecks.\n\nThe previous attempt with a separate <<<1,1>>> postprocess kernel added about 5-10μs per round. Fusing into the main kernel would save that overhead, but across 280 rounds that's only 1.4-2.8ms total—still below the threshold needed to justify the rollback. The real constraint is that 280 rounds × 75μs per round = 21ms of overhead, and even eliminating all of it barely moves the needle for the APC 300 rollback threshold. The implementation would also be complex, requiring CUDA kernel modifications and GPU-side Poseidon2 operations with careful verification.\n\nI should stop chasing marginal optimizations and focus on the approach with the strongest evidence. Hoisting the MLE round per-trace main_ptrs construction out of the loop completely eliminates per-round host-to-device transfers for main_ptrs. The profiling data shows H2D calls dropping from 30,035 to 24,436 when batching per round, but full hoisting would eliminate all ~24,000+ per-trace uploads entirely, potentially saving 10-20ms compared to the 5ms the batching approach achieved.\n\nHowever, the earlier batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload report indicated the improvement was only 5ms, and even complete elimination would save at most 10ms—right at the rollback threshold for STARK excluding trace. The report also noted that per-call CUDA API overhead is around 1 microsecond, with kernel execution time being the dominant cost, not API overhead. Given these constraints, I'll frame this as a concrete optimization task with built-in instrumentation to measure the exact overhead contribution, and proceed with the hoisting approach if the measurements justify it.\n\nThe optimization targets the MLE Rounds overhead by moving the per-trace main_ptrs DeviceBuffer construction and upload outside the prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu loop, eliminating redundant allocation and host-to-device transfers for data that remains constant across rounds.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null