{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MWqxuCnHakpx4cuV3scEW8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Hoist MLE Trace Context Construction\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce MLE Rounds overhead by pre-allocating per-trace device pointer buffers (`main_ptrs_dev`) ONCE before the MLE round loop and reusing them via `copy_to()` instead of `to_device()` (which allocates + copies) each round. At APC 300, MLE Rounds = 163ms with ~113ms kernel time and ~50ms overhead across 28 round iterations (~1.79ms/round). A previous optimization (\"batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload\", 2026-04-13-1430) confirmed per-trace uploads contribute measurable overhead — it saved 5ms by batching 2 uploads per round instead of ~5600 per-trace uploads. This plan completes that work by eliminating the per-round ALLOCATION component: pre-allocate device buffers once, then only COPY updated pointer values each round.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, function `compute_batch_s_poly_eval_and_interp()` (called once per MLE round)\n10\t\n11\t### Per-round trace context construction (lines ~1780-1841):\n12\t\n13\tFor each Case B (\"early\") trace (num_y > 1), the function:\n14\t\n15\t1. Computes `sels_ptr`, `prep_ptr` from the interpolation buffer offset\n16\t2. Builds `main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` from per-matrix pointers into the interpolation buffer (lines 1812-1822)\n17\t3. Calls `main_ptrs.to_device()` (line 1824) → allocates DeviceBuffer + H2D copy\n18\t4. Pushes `TraceCtx` to `early_eval` vector (lines 1826-1840)\n19\t\n20\tFor each Case A (\"late\") trace (num_y = 1), similar construction with `main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n21\t\n22\t### Allocation pattern at APC 300:\n23\t\n24\t- ~312 traces per segment, ~14 rounds per segment\n25\t- Early rounds: most traces are Case B → ~300 `to_device()` calls per round\n26\t- Late rounds: most traces transition to Case A → ~300 `to_device()` calls still\n27\t- Total per segment: ~300 × 14 = ~4,200 `to_device()` calls (each = cudaMallocAsync + cudaMemcpyAsync)\n28\t- Total per proof: ~8,400 allocation+upload cycles\n29\t- Each cycle: ~1.5μs (cudaMallocAsync ~1μs + cudaMemcpyAsync ~0.3μs + cudaFreeAsync ~0.2μs on drop)\n30\t- Total overhead: ~8,400 × 1.5μs ≈ 12.6ms\n31\t\n32\t### `to_device()` implementation (from `crates/cuda-common/src/copy.rs` line 89):\n33\t```rust\n34\tfn to_device(&self) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<T>, MemCopyError> {\n35\t    let mut dst = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(self.len());  // cudaMallocAsync\n36\t    self.copy_to(&mut dst)?;                                  // cudaMemcpyAsync\n37\t    Ok(dst)\n38\t}\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\tEach `to_device()` allocates a fresh DeviceBuffer. On drop (when TraceCtx is dropped at end of round), `cudaFreeAsync` is called. Pre-allocating the buffer and using `copy_to()` directly eliminates the alloc+free pair.\n42\t\n43\t## Changes\n44\t\n45\t### Change 1: Pre-allocate per-trace main_ptrs DeviceBuffers before the MLE loop\n46\t\n47\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n48\t\n49\t**Where**: Before the MLE sumcheck round loop (approximately line 1648, inside `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu`)\n50\t\n51\t**What**: For each trace, allocate a `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` at the maximum needed size (number of main matrices for that trace). Store in a `Vec<DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>>` indexed by trace_idx. The maximum needed size per trace is known from `self.trace_metas` — it's the count of matrices in `mat_evals_per_trace[trace_idx]` minus the preprocessed matrix (if any).\n52\t\n53\t```rust\n54\t// Pre-allocate device buffers for per-trace main_ptrs, indexed by trace_idx\n55\tlet d_main_ptrs_pool: Vec<DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>> = self.trace_metas\n56\t    .iter()\n57\t    .map(|meta| {\n58\t        let first_main_idx = usize::from(meta.has_preprocessed);\n59\t        let num_main_mats = self.mat_evals_per_trace[meta.trace_idx].len() - first_main_idx;\n60\t        DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(num_main_mats)\n61\t    })\n62\t    .collect();\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\t**Why**: Each DeviceBuffer is allocated once (total ~312 allocations for APC 300). Across 14 MLE rounds, this replaces ~4,200 allocation+free pairs per segment with ~312 one-time allocations + ~4,200 copy-only operations. Net savings: ~4,200 × 1.0μs (alloc overhead) + ~4,200 × 0.2μs (free overhead) ≈ 5ms per segment, ~10ms per proof.\n66\t\n67\t### Change 2: Replace to_device() with copy_to() in TraceCtx construction\n68\t\n69\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines ~1812-1824\n70\t\n71\t**What**: Instead of `let main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;`, use `main_ptrs.copy_to(&mut d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx])?;` and then reference `&d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx]` in the TraceCtx.\n72\t\n73\t**Before**:\n74\t```rust\n75\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // allocate + copy\n76\tearly_eval.push(TraceCtx {\n77\t    // ...\n78\t    main_ptrs_dev,\n79\t    // ...\n80\t});\n81\t```\n82\t\n83\t**After**:\n84\t```rust\n85\tmain_ptrs.copy_to(&mut d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx])?;  // copy only\n86\tearly_eval.push(TraceCtx {\n87\t    // ...\n88\t    main_ptrs_dev: unsafe {\n89\t        DeviceBuffer::non_owning(\n90\t            d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx].as_mut_ptr(),\n91\t            main_ptrs.len(),\n92\t        )\n93\t    },\n94\t    // ...\n95\t});\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\tThe non-owning view ensures TraceCtx doesn't free the pre-allocated buffer when dropped.\n99\t\n100\t**Why**: The copy_to() operation is just `cudaMemcpyAsync` (~0.3μs for 40 bytes), eliminating the `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaFreeAsync` pair.\n101\t\n102\t### Change 3: Apply the same pattern to Case A (late) trace context construction\n103\t\n104\t**File**: Same file, similar code block for late_eval traces\n105\t\n106\t**What**: Same transformation — use pre-allocated d_main_ptrs_pool instead of fresh to_device() calls.\n107\t\n108\t### Change 4: Ensure d_main_ptrs_pool lifetime spans the entire MLE loop\n109\t\n110\t**File**: Same file\n111\t\n112\t**What**: The `d_main_ptrs_pool` Vec must be declared BEFORE the MLE round loop and dropped AFTER it. The non-owning DeviceBuffers in TraceCtx must not outlive the pool. Since early_eval/late_eval vectors are rebuilt (and their old entries dropped) each round, this is satisfied as long as the pool outlives the loop.\n113\t\n114\t## Invariants\n115\t\n116\t1. **Pointer correctness**: The `main_ptrs` host vectors are rebuilt each round with correct pointers (into the interpolation buffer for Case B traces, into fold buffers for Case A traces). The `copy_to()` uploads the fresh pointer values to the pre-allocated device buffer. The CUDA kernel reads the updated pointers.\n117\t\n118\t2. **Buffer lifetime**: `d_main_ptrs_pool` must outlive all non-owning TraceCtx views. The pool is declared before the loop and dropped after, so this is guaranteed.\n119\t\n120\t3. **No double-free**: TraceCtx contains non-owning DeviceBuffers (`owns_memory: false`). When TraceCtx is dropped at end of round, the device memory is NOT freed. The pool frees it after the loop.\n121\t\n122\t4. **Size consistency**: Each pre-allocated buffer has capacity = num_main_matrices for that trace. This doesn't change between rounds (the number of matrices per trace is fixed). The `copy_to()` copies exactly `main_ptrs.len()` elements, which equals num_main_matrices.\n123\t\n124\t5. **No aliasing**: Each trace has its own entry in d_main_ptrs_pool. Different traces are never assigned the same buffer.\n125\t\n126\t## Measurement Plan\n127\t\n128\tRun `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` from the powdr repo for APC {0, 100, 300}.\n129\t\n130\tKey metrics:\n131\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300: currently 163ms, target ≤ 153ms (10ms improvement)\n132\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300: currently 1106ms, target ≤ 1096ms\n133\t- No regression at APC 0 (MLE: 118ms, STARK: 1797ms)\n134\t\n135\tNsight verification:\n136\t- cudaMallocAsync call count should drop by ~8,000-10,000\n137\t- cudaFreeAsync call count should drop similarly\n138\t- cudaMemcpyAsync count should stay similar (copies still happen, just without allocation)\n139\t\n140\t## Rollback Criteria\n141\t\n142\tRevert if:\n143\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by less than 10ms\n144\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by less than 10ms\n145\t- Any APC 0 regression exceeds 20ms on STARK excl trace\n146\t- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n147\t\n148\tNote: The previous \"batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload\" (2026-04-13-1430) saved 5ms by batching uploads per round. This plan targets the ALLOCATION component that the batch approach didn't address (it still allocated fresh buffers per batch). If measured improvement is below 10ms, it confirms that cudaMallocAsync pool overhead is genuinely ~0.5μs per call (as reported), and the MLE overhead is dominated by other sources.\n149\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FEaHnKTkJi1aZs7VhUfxJN","session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"eb44796f-7013-49ae-a903-331d1785f55d","timestamp":"2026-04-15T23:13:17.423Z"}
