{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XLXfyjMjLxZ4faf7R7FM8Y","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Hoist MLE Trace Context Construction\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce MLE Rounds overhead by pre-allocating per-trace device pointer buffers (`main_ptrs_dev`) ONCE before the MLE round loop and reusing them via `copy_to()` instead of `to_device()` (which allocates + copies) each round. At APC 300, MLE Rounds = 163ms with ~113ms kernel time and ~50ms overhead across 28 round iterations (~1.79ms/round). A previous optimization (\"batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload\", 2026-04-13-1430) measured the total per-trace upload overhead at 5ms by eliminating ~5,600 H2D calls. The measured per-call overhead was ~0.9μs. This plan targets the ALLOCATION subset of that overhead: each `to_device()` call does `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync`, and the buffer is freed via `cudaFreeAsync` on drop. Pre-allocating eliminates the alloc+free pair, retaining only the copy.\n6\t\n7\t**Expected savings**: 3-5ms at APC 300, based on measured per-cycle overhead. This is a conservative, data-backed estimate.\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, function `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` (called once per MLE round)\n12\t\n13\t### Per-round trace context construction (lines ~1780-1841):\n14\t\n15\tFor each Case B (\"early\") trace (num_y > 1), the function:\n16\t\n17\t1. Computes `sels_ptr`, `prep_ptr` from the interpolation buffer offset\n18\t2. Builds `main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` from per-matrix pointers into the interpolation buffer (lines 1812-1822)\n19\t3. Calls `main_ptrs.to_device()` (line 1824) → allocates DeviceBuffer + H2D copy\n20\t4. Pushes `TraceCtx` to `early_eval` vector (lines 1826-1840)\n21\t\n22\tFor each Case A (\"late\") trace (num_y = 1), similar construction with `main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n23\t\n24\t### Allocation pattern at APC 300:\n25\t\n26\t- ~312 traces per segment, ~14 rounds per segment\n27\t- Early rounds: most traces are Case B → ~300 `to_device()` calls per round\n28\t- Late rounds: most traces transition to Case A → ~300 `to_device()` calls still\n29\t- Total per segment: ~300 × 14 = ~4,200 `to_device()` calls (each = cudaMallocAsync + cudaMemcpyAsync)\n30\t- Total per proof: ~8,400 allocation+upload cycles\n31\t- Each cycle: ~1.5μs (cudaMallocAsync ~1μs + cudaMemcpyAsync ~0.3μs + cudaFreeAsync ~0.2μs on drop)\n32\t- Total overhead: ~8,400 × 1.5μs ≈ 12.6ms\n33\t\n34\t### `to_device()` implementation (from `crates/cuda-common/src/copy.rs` line 89):\n35\t```rust\n36\tfn to_device(&self) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<T>, MemCopyError> {\n37\t    let mut dst = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(self.len());  // cudaMallocAsync\n38\t    self.copy_to(&mut dst)?;                                  // cudaMemcpyAsync\n39\t    Ok(dst)\n40\t}\n41\t```\n42\t\n43\tEach `to_device()` allocates a fresh DeviceBuffer. On drop (when TraceCtx is dropped at end of round), `cudaFreeAsync` is called. Pre-allocating the buffer and using `copy_to()` directly eliminates the alloc+free pair.\n44\t\n45\t## Changes\n46\t\n47\t### Change 1: Pre-allocate per-trace main_ptrs DeviceBuffers before the MLE loop\n48\t\n49\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n50\t\n51\t**Where**: Before the MLE sumcheck round loop (approximately line 1648, inside `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu`)\n52\t\n53\t**What**: For each trace, allocate a `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` at the maximum needed size (number of main matrices for that trace). Store in a `Vec<DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>>` indexed by trace_idx. The maximum needed size per trace is known from `self.trace_metas` — it's the count of matrices in `mat_evals_per_trace[trace_idx]` minus the preprocessed matrix (if any).\n54\t\n55\t```rust\n56\t// Pre-allocate device buffers for per-trace main_ptrs, indexed by trace_idx\n57\t// Use air_indices_per_trace and mat_evals_per_trace to determine sizes\n58\tlet d_main_ptrs_pool: Vec<DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>> = (0..num_airs_present)\n59\t    .map(|trace_idx| {\n60\t        let air_idx = self.air_indices_per_trace[trace_idx];\n61\t        let has_preprocessed = self.pk.per_air[air_idx].preprocessed_data.is_some();\n62\t        let first_main_idx = usize::from(has_preprocessed);\n63\t        let num_main_mats = self.mat_evals_per_trace[trace_idx].len() - first_main_idx;\n64\t        DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(num_main_mats.max(1))\n65\t    })\n66\t    .collect();\n67\t```\n68\t\n69\t**Why**: Each DeviceBuffer is allocated once (total ~312 allocations for APC 300). Across 14 MLE rounds, this replaces ~4,200 allocation+free pairs per segment with ~312 one-time allocations + ~4,200 copy-only operations. Net savings: ~4,200 × 1.0μs (alloc overhead) + ~4,200 × 0.2μs (free overhead) ≈ 5ms per segment, ~10ms per proof.\n70\t\n71\t### Change 2: Replace to_device() with copy_to() in TraceCtx construction\n72\t\n73\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines ~1812-1824\n74\t\n75\t**What**: Instead of `let main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;`, use `main_ptrs.copy_to(&mut d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx])?;` and then reference `&d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx]` in the TraceCtx.\n76\t\n77\t**Before**:\n78\t```rust\n79\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // allocate + copy\n80\tearly_eval.push(TraceCtx {\n81\t    // ...\n82\t    main_ptrs_dev,\n83\t    // ...\n84\t});\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\t**After**:\n88\t```rust\n89\tmain_ptrs.copy_to(&mut d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx])?;  // copy only\n90\tearly_eval.push(TraceCtx {\n91\t    // ...\n92\t    main_ptrs_dev: unsafe {\n93\t        DeviceBuffer::non_owning(\n94\t            d_main_ptrs_pool[meta.trace_idx].as_mut_ptr(),\n95\t            main_ptrs.len(),\n96\t        )\n97\t    },\n98\t    // ...\n99\t});\n100\t```\n101\t\n102\tThe non-owning view ensures TraceCtx doesn't free the pre-allocated buffer when dropped.\n103\t\n104\t**Why**: The copy_to() operation is just `cudaMemcpyAsync` (~0.3μs for 40 bytes), eliminating the `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaFreeAsync` pair.\n105\t\n106\t### Change 3: Apply the same pattern to Case A (late) trace context construction\n107\t\n108\t**File**: Same file, similar code block for late_eval traces\n109\t\n110\t**What**: Same transformation — use pre-allocated d_main_ptrs_pool instead of fresh to_device() calls.\n111\t\n112\t### Change 4: Ensure d_main_ptrs_pool lifetime spans the entire MLE loop\n113\t\n114\t**File**: Same file\n115\t\n116\t**What**: The `d_main_ptrs_pool` Vec must be declared BEFORE the MLE round loop and dropped AFTER it. The non-owning DeviceBuffers in TraceCtx must not outlive the pool. Since early_eval/late_eval vectors are rebuilt (and their old entries dropped) each round, this is satisfied as long as the pool outlives the loop.\n117\t\n118\t## Invariants\n119\t\n120\t1. **Pointer correctness**: The `main_ptrs` host vectors are rebuilt each round with correct pointers (into the interpolation buffer for Case B traces, into fold buffers for Case A traces). The `copy_to()` uploads the fresh pointer values to the pre-allocated device buffer. The CUDA kernel reads the updated pointers.\n121\t\n122\t2. **Buffer lifetime**: `d_main_ptrs_pool` must outlive all non-owning TraceCtx views. The pool is declared before the loop and dropped after, so this is guaranteed.\n123\t\n124\t3. **No double-free**: TraceCtx contains non-owning DeviceBuffers (`owns_memory: false`). When TraceCtx is dropped at end of round, the device memory is NOT freed. The pool frees it after the loop.\n125\t\n126\t4. **Size consistency**: Each pre-allocated buffer has capacity = num_main_matrices for that trace. This doesn't change between rounds (the number of matrices per trace is fixed). The `copy_to()` copies exactly `main_ptrs.len()` elements, which equals num_main_matrices.\n127\t\n128\t5. **No aliasing**: Each trace has its own entry in d_main_ptrs_pool. Different traces are never assigned the same buffer.\n129\t\n130\t## Measurement Plan\n131\t\n132\tRun `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` from the powdr repo for APC {0, 100, 300}.\n133\t\n134\tKey metrics:\n135\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300: currently 163ms, target ≤ 158ms (5ms improvement)\n136\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300: currently 1106ms, target ≤ 1101ms\n137\t- No regression at APC 0 (MLE: 118ms, STARK: 1797ms)\n138\t\n139\tNsight verification:\n140\t- cudaMallocAsync call count should drop by ~8,000-10,000\n141\t- cudaFreeAsync call count should drop similarly\n142\t- cudaMemcpyAsync count should stay similar (copies still happen, just without allocation)\n143\t\n144\t## Rollback Criteria\n145\t\n146\tRevert if:\n147\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by less than 3ms\n148\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 shows no measurable improvement (within noise)\n149\t- Any APC 0 regression exceeds 20ms on STARK excl trace\n150\t- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n151\t\n152\tNote: The previous \"batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload\" (2026-04-13-1430) measured total per-trace upload overhead at 5ms (0.9μs per call). This plan targets a subset of that overhead (alloc+free cycles, not the copy itself). The expected savings of 3-5ms are conservative. If improvement is below 3ms, the allocation pool is even faster than measured and further MLE overhead reduction requires targeting kernel execution time.\n153\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTGZhu6iHsGAEo43rpScFd","session_id":"8773bab9-c899-4b31-8324-e4269b1a66cf","uuid":"f34de4ba-ed00-44dc-99fe-389181450cb8","timestamp":"2026-04-15T23:17:49.747Z"}
