ould roughly halve the number of pool entries needed and simplify the `non_owning` lifetime management, with negligible impact on total savings.\n\n### Use a single flat buffer instead of per-trace entries\n\nInstead of `Vec<DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>>` with one allocation per trace, a single `DeviceBuffer` with computed offsets (the same pattern used for `_keepalive_interpolated` at line 1736) would reduce the pre-allocation to a single `cudaMallocAsync` call. The per-trace element counts are small (1-5 `MainMatrixPtrs` per trace), so the total flat buffer would be ~1500 elements -- trivial. This matches an existing pattern in the codebase and avoids 312 separate `cudaMallocAsync` calls during pre-allocation.\n\n## Assessment\n\n**No remaining design-level blocker.** The mechanism is correct: pre-allocating device buffers and replacing `to_device()` with `copy_to()` eliminates the per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` pairs. The `non_owning` pattern is already used in the codebase (`base.rs:70`, `mod.rs:2131`). The data structures and invariants are sound.\n\n**The plan is prototype-ready**, with one required clarification: the pre-allocation must be placed as a prover struct field or as a local in `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu` (around line 647), not at \"line 1648\" which is inside `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`. An implementer familiar with the codebase can resolve this without guessing -- the existing ping-pong buffer allocation block at lines 622-646 is the natural placement site.\n\n**The strongest part of the plan** is its honesty about expected savings. The 3-5ms target is grounded in the measured 0.9us per-call overhead from the previous batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload task, and correctly identifies that it targets only the allocation subset of that overhead. The arithmetic (eliminating ~8,400 alloc+free pairs at ~0.5-1.0us each) supports the 3-5ms range.\n\n**The 3ms rollback threshold is tight but defensible.** It is at the noise floor for a single 